  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nMost areas should see primarily clear skies through Saturday despite \na dry cold front crossing the area today, and a shortwave impacting \nparts of the northwest forecast area late Thursday night into Friday \nmorning. Today's front should bring not much more than an increased \nlight southwesterly breeze of 10 to 15 mph with gusts around 20 mph, \nshifting to northwesterly this afternoon (evening south of I-70), \nand decreasing overnight. High pressure will build back in for \nThursday, and shift toward the northeast U.S. Friday, bringing light \nwinds and clear skies for Thursday, but allowing a shortwave and \nassociated warm advection to bring a slight chance for showers \nlate Thursday night into Friday morning, mainly north of I-74.\n\nThe next feature of note will arrive Saturday night as remnants of \nTropical Storm Isaiahs, currently over the Gulf of America, arrives \nin the Ohio Valley region. The National Hurricane Center track shows \nthis feature arriving as a tropical depression near the SW tip \nof IN at 7 AM Sunday, although models and official NHC track \nindicate continuing track uncertainty on the order of a state \nwidth. 00Z-LREF ensemble forecast now depicts 40 to 60 percent \nchances for more than half inch rainfall south of I-70, and up \nto 25 percent chance for over an inch, offering hope for a \nbeneficial rainfall in this area but very low chances for \nexcessive rainfall this far north. To the northwest of I-70, \nchances for greater than a quarter inch are less 50 percent and \nchances for any precipitation are less than 10 percent northwest\nof the IL River. Ensemble maximum precipitation is less than 2 \ninches across central and southeast IL.\n\nIsaias should take a rapid turn eastward as it reaches the Ohio \nValley region, with precipitation ending by Sunday afternoon across \ncentral and southeast IL. Dry conditions are forecast for Monday, \nfollowed by slight chances for precipitation again Tuesday as \nanother frontal system passes through the region, likely with most \nof its impacts over the upper Midwest and Great Lakes.\n\nTemperatures will follow a warming trend through Saturday as highs \nrise to the upper 70s and lower 80s Thursday, increasing to mid and \nupper 80s by Saturday. A dip in temperatures is forecast Sunday as \ncloud cover and precipitation associated with Isaias affect the \narea, and highs should be mainly mid 70s. Mid 80s can be expected \nMonday, followed by around 10 to 15 degrees of cooling for \nmidweek associated with the frontal system.",
